\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.21}{\ignorespaces Backend engineering decomposition of CAVIX. The diagram inventories the FastAPI route buckets (auth, live-ops, investigation, response, intelligence), the 24 stateless service classes grouped by domain, the repository layer that wraps the 19 ORM tables, the adapter layer that isolates external dependencies (model loaders, preprocessing, external APIs, Redis, AI clients), the seven worker processes managed through \texttt  {Procfile.host}, the five SHA-pinned model artefacts under \texttt  {apps/models/}, and the observability stack (structlog, Prometheus, OpenTelemetry). The four prose components of section~\ref {sec:platform-components} map across this picture: the Detection Engine corresponds to the inference worker plus model loaders plus the five model artefacts; the Wazuh Integration corresponds to \texttt  {wazuh\_bridge} plus the Wazuh adapter; the Threat Intelligence Module corresponds to \texttt  {intel\_feeds\_worker} plus the intel-domain services and routes; the Analyst Dashboard corresponds to the web and mobile clients plus the live-ops routes and services.}}{81}{figure.4.21}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:ch4:components}{{4.21}{81}{Backend engineering decomposition of CAVIX. The diagram inventories the FastAPI route buckets (auth, live-ops, investigation, response, intelligence), the 24 stateless service classes grouped by domain, the repository layer that wraps the 19 ORM tables, the adapter layer that isolates external dependencies (model loaders, preprocessing, external APIs, Redis, AI clients), the seven worker processes managed through \texttt {Procfile.host}, the five SHA-pinned model artefacts under \texttt {apps/models/}, and the observability stack (structlog, Prometheus, OpenTelemetry). The four prose components of section~\ref {sec:platform-components} map across this picture: the Detection Engine corresponds to the inference worker plus model loaders plus the five model artefacts; the Wazuh Integration corresponds to \texttt {wazuh\_bridge} plus the Wazuh adapter; the Threat Intelligence Module corresponds to \texttt {intel\_feeds\_worker} plus the intel-domain services and routes; the Analyst Dashboard corresponds to the web and mobile clients plus the live-ops routes and services}{figure.4.21}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.22}{\ignorespaces The full data flow. Stages are numbered on the left for reference in the rest of this section. Stream keys (orange dashed boxes) are Redis Streams \cite {redis2024}; workers (green) are separate Python processes managed through \texttt  {Procfile.host} or the Compose worker profile; the inference worker hosts the five-model ensemble. The DQN action is gated downstream by deterministic thresholds, marked with an asterisk and explained in figure~\ref {fig:ch4:dqngating}.}}{82}{figure.4.22}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:ch4:dataflowmaster}{{4.22}{82}{The full data flow. Stages are numbered on the left for reference in the rest of this section. Stream keys (orange dashed boxes) are Redis Streams \protect \cite {redis2024}; workers (green) are separate Python processes managed through \texttt {Procfile.host} or the Compose worker profile; the inference worker hosts the five-model ensemble. The DQN action is gated downstream by deterministic thresholds, marked with an asterisk and explained in figure~\ref {fig:ch4:dqngating}}{figure.4.22}{}}
